## Standard Library

### console

```redblue
say "Hello"                      // print with a newline
print "Hello"                    // print without one
console.log("Hello")             // `say` under its module spelling
console.error("Could not read")  // diagnostics go to stderr
console.clear()                  // clear the screen
set name to input("Your name? ")  // read one line from the terminal
```

`say` and `print` are keywords rather than module functions, so there is no
`console.say` and no `console.print`: a module has the functions that are spelled
`<module>_<member>` in the registry, and those two are not. `console.log` is the
module spelling of `say` and answers `nothing`, so `say console.log("x")` prints
`x` and then `nothing`. `console.error` writes to stderr rather than stdout, so a
program that collects its output can keep its diagnostics separate from it.
`console.clear` writes the terminal's clear sequence to stdout and answers
`nothing`; on a stream that is not a terminal the sequence is still written.

`input` is the whole of the language's input: it writes its first argument as a
prompt, reads one line from the terminal, and answers that line as text with the
line terminator removed — `"abc"` from `abc\n` and from `abc` alike, since a pipe
whose last line has no newline is still a line. At the end of the input it
answers the empty text rather than refusing, so a program that runs out of input
sees an empty answer instead of an error. The prompt is optional:
`input()` reads a line and asks nothing. It is a registered builtin rather than a
module function, so there is no `console.input` — the same rule as for `say` and
`print` — and it takes parentheses like any other call.

There is no `ask`. It was a reserved word with no rule in the parser to read it,
which is how this section came to name it, and it is an ordinary identifier
again — `set ask to 1` is a program. Both spellings are refused: `ask("n")` is
`Unknown function 'ask'`, and `ask "n"` — which is not a call at all, because a
function is only called with parentheses — is `Unknown variable 'ask'`.

### text

```redblue
set upper to text.uppercase("hello")  // "HELLO"
set lower to text.lowercase("HELLO")  // "hello"
set trimmed to text.trim("  hi  ")    // "hi"
set parts to text.split("a,b,c", by ",")  // ["a", "b", "c"]
set joined to text.join(["a", "b", "c"], by ",")  // "a,b,c"
set holds to text.contains("abc", "b")  // yes
set starts to text.starts_with("abc", "ab")  // yes
set ends to text.ends_with("abc", "bc")  // yes
set swapped to text.replace("a-b", "-", "+")  // "a+b"
...
on a pattern and leaves a text whole when the pattern does not match. An empty
pattern is a valid pattern and matches at position zero.

A pattern that does not compile is a catchable `Runtime` failure carrying the
crate's message; it is never a panic. The `regex` crate is linear-time, so a
pattern that backtracks catastrophically in another engine cannot hang a
Redblue program. `text.split` is unchanged: its separator is still literal, and
`regex.split` is the pattern form.

## Iteration and memoisation

### Iteration helpers

The helpers below take lists and answer lists. None of them is lazy: Redblue has
no iterator value, so there is nothing for a lazy helper to hand back, and each
one builds and returns a new list when it is called. The table says so per
helper because on a list-valued language that has to be written down rather
than assumed.

| Helper | Answer | Lazy? |
|---|---|---|
| `chain(a, b, ...)` | materialises a list of every argument list's items in the order given; no lists is the empty list | materialises |
| `zip(a, b, ...)` | materialises `[a-item, b-item, ...]` rows, one item from each list; it takes at least two lists and **stops at the shorter** input, never padding with `nothing` | materialises |
| `enumerate(xs)` | materialises `[index, item]` pairs with indexes from zero | materialises |
| `product(a, b)` | materialises every `[a-item, b-item]` combination, first input outermost | materialises |
| `groupby(xs)` | materialises `{key, items}` records, in first-appearance order, grouping equal keys together | materialises |
| `take(xs, n)` | materialises the first `n` items; `n` below zero is empty and past the end is the whole list; a positive fractional `n` is refused | materialises |
| `skip(xs, n)` | materialises the list without its first `n` items, bounded the same way; a positive fractional `n` is refused | materialises |

```redblue
set both to chain([1, 2], [3])                  // [1, 2, 3]
set pairs to zip([1, 2], ["a", "b"])            // [[1, a], [2, b]]
set rows to zip([1, 2], ["a", "b"], [yes, no])  // [[1, a, yes], [2, b, no]]
set indexed to enumerate(["a", "b"])            // [[0, a], [1, b]]
set grid to product([1, 2], ["a"])              // [[1, a], [2, a]]
set buckets to groupby([2, 1, 2])               // [{key: 2, items: [2, 2]}, {key: 1, items: [1]}]
set first to take([1, 2, 3], 2)                 // [1, 2]
set rest to skip([1, 2, 3], 2)                  // [3]
```

`groupby` compares keys with Redblue equality, so two equal lists or records
share a group. Its groups are ordered by where their key first appeared, not by
the key's value, so `groupby([2, 1, 2])` lists the `2` group first. A keyed
lookup finds a group, so the work grows with the input rather than comparing
every item with every earlier key.

`product` materialises at most 1,000,000 pairs in one call. A call whose answer
would be longer is refused with a resource-limit failure instead of trying to
build it: the step budget charges a builtin call once, so it cannot be the guard
for a result whose length is the product of its two inputs.

### Memoisation

A function declaration may be decorated with an LRU cache:

```redblue
lru_cache(128) to fib(n)
    if n < 2 then
        give back n
    end
    give back fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)
end
```

The capacity is a whole number of at least one and may be omitted, in which case
it is 128. A call whose arguments equal an earlier call's arguments answers from
the cache without running the body; a miss runs the body and records its answer.
A function value among the arguments is matched by identity rather than by the
language's `is`: two declarations can produce functions with the same name,
parameters and captures but different bodies, and neither may answer from the
other's entry. The same rule reaches a function nested in a list or a record.
When the cache is full, the least recently used entry is evicted. Only successful
answers are recorded, so a failed call is tried again. The cache belongs to the
function value the declaration produces, not to one spelling of calling it or to
its name: a direct call and an element of `map` all consult the same memory and
answer a hit without running the body. The decorator is written on a plain `to`
=== README tail ===
| `constant PI to 3.14159` | module-level constant |

Full grammar: [`docs/GRAMMAR.md`](docs/GRAMMAR.md). Design rationale: [`PHILOSOPHY.md`](PHILOSOPHY.md). Specification: [`SPEC.md`](SPEC.md). Library policy — what is written in Redblue, what is a Rust binding, and what is not built: [`LIBRARIES.md`](LIBRARIES.md).

## Standard Library

Before adding a module or builtin, read [`LIBRARIES.md`](LIBRARIES.md): it is the
standing decision on whether a capability is written in Redblue, bound to a Rust
crate, or not built at all.

### Files
```
files.read(path)      // Read file as text
files.write(path, content)
files.append(path, content)
files.exists(path)   // Check if file exists
files.lines(path)    // Read as list of lines
files.delete(path)
files.copy(from, to)     // Copy a file
files.rename(from, to)   // Rename it, or move it
```

### Bytes
```
bytes.from_text(text)   // Text to a list of byte values (0 to 255)
bytes.text(bytes)       // A byte list back to text
bytes.write(path, bytes) // Write a byte list to a file
```

### Time
```
time.now()             // Get current timestamp
time.sleep(seconds) // Sleep for N seconds
time.format(timestamp_or_date, "format")
time.parse("YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS+HH:MM")         // ISO-8601 text to a date
time.parse("29/02/2024", "%d/%m/%Y")            // text in a chosen layout
time.unix("YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS")
time.from_unix(seconds)                        // Seconds to a date, read as UTC
time.date(year, month, day)                    // A date at midnight
time.datetime(year, month, day, hour, minute, second)
time.duration(seconds)                         // A signed span of time
time.add_years(date, years)                    // Calendar arithmetic
time.add_months(date, months)                  // Clamps at month end
time.add_days(date, days)                      // Fixed 24-hour days
time.add_hours(date, hours)
time.add_minutes(date, minutes)
time.add_seconds(date, seconds)
// dates compare with < <= > >=, subtract to a duration, and add a duration
```

### Sys
```
sys.argv()             // Arguments after the program path (empty outside `rb run`)
```

### Network
```
network.get(url)     // HTTP GET
network.post(url, body)
```

### JSON and CSV
```
json.parse(text)     // Parse JSON to record
json.stringify(value)
csv.parse(text)     // Parse CSV to list of lists
```

### Math
```
PI, E                          // Constants, not module members: no math.PI
math.abs(x), math.floor(x), math.ceil(x)
math.round(x), math.sqrt(x), math.pow(x, y)
math.sin(x), math.cos(x), math.tan(x)
math.log(x), math.exp(x)
// every one of them is also spelled flat: abs(x), sqrt(x), ...
// math.random is not implemented, and there is no math.random
```

### Text
```
text.uppercase("text"), text.lowercase("text"), text.trim("text")
text.split("text", by), text.join(list, by)
text.contains("text", sub)
text.starts_with("text", prefix), text.ends_with("text", suffix)
text.replace("text", from, to)
text.length("text")      // characters, not bytes: text.length("héllo") is 5
// every one of them is also spelled flat: uppercase("text"), split("text", by), ...
```

### Console
```
say "Hello"                      // print with a newline
print "Hello"                    // print without one
console.log("Hello")             // `say` under its module spelling
console.error("Could not read")  // diagnostics go to stderr
console.clear()                  // clear the screen
set name to input("Your name? ")  // read one line from the terminal
```

### List
```
list.map(xs, to (x) give back x * 2)  // every member, through a function
list.length([1, 2, 3])                 // 3
length(xs), push(xs, x)                // flat list builtins
```

### Regex
```
regex.match(text, pattern)             // a match record, or nothing
regex.is_match(text, pattern)          // yes/no, searching anywhere
regex.capture(text, pattern, group)    // a captured text, or nothing
regex.replace(text, pattern, "user-$1")// every match, with capture references
regex.split(text, pattern)             // the text between matches
// text.split keeps its literal separator; regex.split is the pattern form.
```

